---
import MenuEN from '../../../components/MenuEN.astro';
import Footer from '../../../components/Footer.astro';
import Analytics from '../../../components/Analytics.astro';
import Seo from '../../../components/Seo.astro';
import { blogPostingJsonLd } from '../../../i18n/jsonld.js';
import '../../../sass/general.scss';
---

<!DOCTYPE html>
<html lang="en">
<head><meta charset="UTF-8">
    <link rel="icon" href="/favicon.ico" />
    <meta name="viewport" content="width=device-width, initial-scale=1.0">
    <link href="https://fonts.googleapis.com/css2?family=Inter:wght@400;500;600;700&family=Shrikhand&display=swap" rel="stylesheet">
    <Seo page="blogSpotVideo" lang="en" type="article"
        title="Bilene on video: a guided tour of the kitesurf spot"
        description="Two minutes of drone footage over the lagoon: the kitesurf spot in Bilene, the Speed Spot, the activities and the evenings — told on video."
        image="/images/1-spot/10.webp">
      <script type="application/ld+json" set:html={JSON.stringify(blogPostingJsonLd('blogSpotVideo', 'en', {
        headline: 'Bilene on video: a guided tour of the kitesurf spot',
        description: 'Two minutes of drone footage over the lagoon: the kitesurf spot in Bilene, the Speed Spot, the activities and the evenings — told on video.',
        image: '/images/1-spot/10.webp',
        datePublished: '2026-07-17',
      }))} />
    </Seo>
    <Analytics />
</head>
<body>
    <MenuEN page="blogSpotVideo" />

    <main>
        <div class="blog-hero">
            <img src="/images/1-spot/10.webp" alt="The Bilene lagoon seen from a drone, white sand beach and turquoise water">
            <div class="blog-hero-overlay">
                <span class="blog-date">July 2026</span>
                <h1>Bilene on video: a guided tour of the spot</h1>
            </div>
        </div>

        <article class="blog-article">
            <p>
                We could describe the Bilene lagoon to you over three pages. We actually did: a whole page about the spot,
                another about the activities, a third about the evenings. Let's be honest for a second — <em>nobody</em> reads
                all that before picking their next kite destination.
            </p>
            <p>
                So we filmed it instead. A drone, a few minutes, and the spot speaking for itself. The video did rather well on
                Instagram, which settles the argument once and for all: our paragraphs will never compete with a wide shot over
                15 km of empty turquoise water.
            </p>

            <a class="blog-reel" href="https://www.instagram.com/reel/DaxR9OluW0P/" target="_blank" rel="noopener">
                <img src="/images/1-spot/13.webp" alt="The Bilene lagoon seen from a drone — watch the video on Instagram">
                <span class="blog-reel-play" aria-hidden="true">▶</span>
                <span class="blog-reel-cta">Watch the video on Instagram</span>
            </a>
            <p class="blog-caption">
                The video is narrated in French — translated versions are on the way. In the meantime, the images need no subtitles.
            </p>

            <h2>15 km of lagoon, closed off from the ocean</h2>
            <p>
                This is what the drone shows in a single shot, and what text takes a paragraph to explain: Bilene is a
                <strong>15 km lagoon, open to the Indian Ocean but shielded from its waves and tides</strong>. A sand bar does the
                work. The result: turquoise water, no swell, no current, and a wind that settles in and holds.
            </p>
            <p>
                For a kiter, that translates simply. More flat water than you know what to do with, downwinders that go on
                forever, direct access to the wave spot on days you fancy a change — and the centre sitting <em>right on the
                beach</em>. You pump, you walk down, you ride. No carrying gear across a car park, no waiting for a slot in a
                crowded riding area.
            </p>
            <p>
                The detail you can't see in the footage: how many people are in the window. There aren't any.
                <a href="/en/the-kitesurf-spot-in-bilene-mozambique/">The spot in full →</a>
            </p>

            <h2>The Speed Spot, twenty minutes upwind</h2>
            <p>
                The moment everyone picked up on in the comments is the mouth of the lagoon. A huge sand bar, perfect flat water
                on one side, the ocean on the other, and nothing around it. You get there <strong>by kite, in about twenty
                minutes</strong> if you're up for it — otherwise by boat.
            </p>
            <p>
                Victor Hayes, who came to ride with us, summed it up better than any of our pages ever did: <em>"it's like our
                freestyle spot in Brazil, except there's nobody here"</em>. It's glassy, it's enormous, and most of the time you
                have it to yourself. If you came for speed or for freestyle on mirror-flat water, that's where it happens.
            </p>

            <h2>Learning here: 1 km² of water, one metre deep</h2>
            <p>
                The teaching area you fly over in the video is hard to find anywhere else: <strong>one square kilometre of warm
                water, one metre deep, with no tide and no current</strong>. You can stand up everywhere, you don't drift, and the
                wind stays steady while you learn to work your window.
            </p>
            <p>
                Lessons are taught by an IKO instructor, with current-year gear, radios, helmets and impact vests. None of that
                shows up on drone footage, but it's the difference between a week of real progress and a week spent walking back
                upwind.
                <a href="/en/kiteschool-in-bilene-mozambique/">The kite school →</a>
            </p>

            <h2>And on days without wind?</h2>
            <p>
                There aren't many, but the video shows what we do with them too. The <strong>Limpopo lighthouse</strong>, the second
                tallest in Africa, at the end of an hour of 4x4 tracks. A boat trip out to the lagoon mouth, home to a family of
                sea turtles — and, between September and November, the whales migrating along the coast. A <strong>braai</strong>
                (whatever you do, don't call it a barbecue here) cooked by a South African by the ocean, after a run through the
                dunes in a 4x4.
            </p>
            <p>
                There's also the Macia market, traditional cooking with Félix, the church singing on Sunday mornings, big game
                fishing, paddling up the rivers that feed into the lagoon. And massages under the palm shelters, facing the sea,
                for legs that have taken a beating.
                <a href="/en/activities-in-bilene-outings-visits-exploration/">All the activities →</a>
            </p>

            <h2>The evenings, once the drone is packed away</h2>
            <p>
                The evening shots at the end aren't staged. The Palmeiras beach bar, feet in the sand, is aperitif headquarters:
                a <strong>2M</strong> — the local beer — and the last riders finishing their session in front of you while you sit
                back in a deckchair.
            </p>
            <p>
                Once a week we fire up the <strong>BKC Team Barbecue</strong>, with the sunset over the lagoon as a backdrop. And
                every other evening we take whoever fancies it to our favourite places in the village: small local restaurants,
                no menu in four languages, where you eat frango com batata and matapa.
                <a href="/en/restaurants-and-dining-in-bilene-mozambique/">Restaurants and evenings out →</a>
            </p>

            <h2>When to come</h2>
            <p>
                The season runs from <strong>September to March</strong>, with an average of <strong>90% of days on the water</strong>.
                The rest of the year it drops to a "miserable" 60%. So the question isn't really whether you'll get to ride.
            </p>
            <p>
                If the video made you want to come, here's what's next: <a href="/en/prepare-your-trip-to-bilene-mozambique/">preparing
                your trip</a> (flights, ETA, transfer, what to pack) and <a href="/en/services-prices-bilene-kitesurf-mozambique/">the
                prices</a>. And if you're still torn between destinations, we made the comparison:
                <a href="/en/blog/new-kitesurf-destinations/">5 new kitesurf destinations with (almost) nobody on them</a>.
            </p>
            <p>See you on the water!</p>

            <a href="/en/blog/" class="blog-back">&larr; Back to the blog</a>
        </article>
    </main>

    <Footer />
</body>
</html>

<style>
    .blog-hero {
        position: relative;
        width: 100%;
        height: 560px;
        overflow: hidden;
    }
    .blog-hero img {
        width: 100%;
        height: 100%;
        object-fit: cover;
    }
    .blog-hero-overlay {
        position: absolute;
        bottom: 0;
        left: 0;
        right: 0;
        padding: 40px 20px;
        background: linear-gradient(transparent, rgba(0,0,0,0.7));
        color: white;
    }
    .blog-hero-overlay h1 {
        font-size: 2.4em;
        margin: 8px 0 0;
        text-shadow: 1px 1px 4px rgba(0,0,0,0.6);
    }
    .blog-hero-overlay .blog-date {
        font-size: 0.9em;
        text-transform: uppercase;
        letter-spacing: 0.1em;
        opacity: 0.9;
    }
    .blog-article {
        max-width: 750px;
        margin: 40px auto;
        padding: 0 20px;
    }
    .blog-article h2 {
        text-align: left;
        font-size: 1.5em;
        margin: 35px 0 12px;
    }
    .blog-article p {
        font-size: 1.05em;
        line-height: 1.8;
        text-align: left;
        color: #444;
        margin-bottom: 15px;
    }
    /* Vignette cliquable vers le reel, et non une iframe : le lecteur Instagram
       ne sert qu'une carte statique et refuse de lire un reel en embed. */
    .blog-reel {
        position: relative;
        display: block;
        max-width: 400px;
        margin: 30px auto 10px;
        aspect-ratio: 9 / 16;
        border-radius: 8px;
        overflow: hidden;
        box-shadow: 0 2px 12px rgba(0,0,0,0.1);
    }
    .blog-reel img {
        display: block;
        width: 100%;
        height: 100%;
        object-fit: cover;
    }
    .blog-reel-play {
        position: absolute;
        top: 50%;
        left: 50%;
        transform: translate(-50%, -50%);
        display: flex;
        align-items: center;
        justify-content: center;
        width: 68px;
        height: 68px;
        padding-left: 5px;
        border-radius: 50%;
        background: rgba(0,0,0,0.55);
        color: white;
        font-size: 1.5em;
        transition: background 0.2s, transform 0.2s;
    }
    .blog-reel:hover .blog-reel-play {
        background: rgba(0,0,0,0.78);
        transform: translate(-50%, -50%) scale(1.08);
    }
    .blog-reel-cta {
        position: absolute;
        bottom: 0;
        left: 0;
        right: 0;
        padding: 34px 15px 14px;
        background: linear-gradient(transparent, rgba(0,0,0,0.75));
        color: white;
        font-weight: bold;
        text-align: center;
    }
    .blog-caption {
        max-width: 400px;
        margin: 0 auto 30px !important;
        font-size: 0.85em !important;
        color: #777 !important;
        text-align: center !important;
        font-style: italic;
    }
    .blog-back {
        display: inline-block;
        margin-top: 20px;
        padding: 10px 20px;
        background: #4695fc;
        color: white;
        border-radius: 5px;
        font-weight: bold;
        transition: background 0.2s;
    }
    .blog-back:hover {
        background: #3578d8;
    }
    .blog-back:visited {
        color: white;
    }
    @media (max-width: 600px) {
        .blog-hero {
            height: 350px;
        }
        .blog-hero-overlay h1 {
            font-size: 1.6em;
        }
    }
</style>
